Phase 21: Docker (Containerize Your Student Portal)

Now that you have:

Plain Text
✅ Flask
✅ SQLite
✅ Login
✅ Search
✅ Student List
✅ CSV Export
✅ Bootstrap
✅ GitHub

The next step is Docker.

Docker lets you package your entire application so it runs anywhere with the same environment.

What is Docker?

Without Docker:

Plain Text
Ubuntu VM
↓
Install Python
↓
Install Flask
↓
Install Dependencies
↓
Run App

With Docker:

Plain Text
docker run studentportal

Everything is already inside the container.

Step 1: Install Docker

Check:

Shell
docker --version

If Docker is not installed:

Shell
sudo apt update
sudo apt install docker.io -y

Start Docker:

Shell
sudo systemctl start docker

Enable Docker:

Shell
sudo systemctl enable docker

Verify:

Shell
docker --version

Example:

Plain Text
Docker version 24.x.x
Step 2: Go To Project
Shell
cd ~/student_portal

Check files:

Shell
ls

Expected:

Plain Text
app.py
students.db
templates
logs
requirements.txt
Step 3: Create requirements.txt

If not already created:

Shell
pip freeze > requirements.txt

Verify:

Shell
cat requirements.txt

Example:

Plain Text
Flask==3.x.x
Step 4: Create Dockerfile

Create:

Shell
nano Dockerfile

Paste:

Dockerfile
FROM python:3.12
 
WORKDIR /app
 
COPY . .
 
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt
 
EXPOSE 5000
 
CMD ["python3", "app.py"]

Save:

Plain Text
Ctrl + O
Enter
Ctrl + X
Step 5: Verify Project Structure
Plain Text
student_portal/
 
├── app.py
├── Dockerfile
├── requirements.txt
├── students.db
├── templates/
├── logs/
└── static/
Step 6: Build Docker Image

Run:

Shell
sudo docker build -t studentportal:v1 .

Explanation:

Plain Text
studentportal = image name
v1 = version
. = current directory
 

Wait for:

Plain Text
Successfully built
Successfully tagged studentportal:v1
Step 7: Verify Image
Shell
sudo docker images

Expected:

Plain Text
REPOSITORY TAG
studentportal v1
Step 8: Run Container

Run:

Shell
sudo docker run -p 5000:5000 studentportal:v1

Explanation:

Plain Text
Host Port Container Port
 
5000 ---> 5000
Step 9: Open Browser

Visit:

Plain Text
http://127.0.0.1:5000

You should see:

Plain Text
Student Portal

🎉 Now Flask is running inside Docker.

Step 10: Check Running Containers

Open another terminal:

Shell
sudo docker ps
 

Example:

Plain Text
CONTAINER ID
IMAGE
PORTS
 
studentportal:v1
Step 11: Stop Container

Find container id:

Shell
sudo docker ps

Then:

Shell
sudo docker stop <container_id>

Example:

Shell
sudo docker stop a1b2c3d4
 
Step 12: Run In Background

Instead of blocking terminal:

Shell
sudo docker run -d -p 5000:5000 studentportal:v1

Now terminal is free.

Check:

Shell
sudo docker ps
Step 13: View Logs
Shell
sudo docker logs <container_id>

Example:

Shell
sudo docker logs a1b2c3d4
Step 14: Enter Container
Shell
sudo docker exec -it <container_id> bash

Example:

Shell
sudo docker exec -it a1b2c3d4 bash

Inside container:

Shell
root@container:/app#

Check files:

Shell
ls
Step 15: Remove Container

List containers:

Shell
sudo docker ps -a

Remove:

Shell
sudo docker rm <container_id>
Step 16: Remove Image
Shell
sudo docker images

Remove:

Shell
sudo docker rmi studentportal:v1
